  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe current SLSQP implementation locates local optima but may remain trapped by poor initialization. This strategy replaces it with multi-resolution optimization and advanced initialization to systematically explore better regions of the solution space. We use parent function interpolation to seed higher resolutions, and apply several specialized initialization techniques to avoid local optima:\n\n1. Fourier resampling: Transforms parent to frequency domain then back with added noise\n2. Polynomial fitting: Interpolates the parent using polynomial functions then projects\n3. Random harmonics: Uses band-restricted Gaussian randomized functions to directly initialize complex shapes\n\nThe algorithm alternates between global and local searches, with each resolution having its own restart strategies and hyperparameter tuning. We implement a unified convergence criterion tracking the last 100 evaluations to escape plateaus.\n\nMathematical specification\nThe optimization domain remains unrestricted except for:\n1. 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n2. \u2211h = n/2 exactly\n\nObjective: Minimize max_k\u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx, evaluated via: max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode=\"full\"))*(2.0/n)\n\nThe solution vector h \u2208 [0,1]^n is subject to above constraints. The core algorithms are:\n- Constrained local search: SLSQP with box constraints\n- Global search: Population-based particle swarm optimization\n- Initialization: Fourier transforms, polynomial basis, harmonic decomposition\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Multi-resolution search (starting from parent):\n   - Candidates: 380, 760, 1520, 3040 points\n   - For each resolution:\n     \u00b3 Three initialization methods:\n        a. Fourier resampling with rectangular window\n        b. Scrambled polynomial regression\n        c. Random harmonic modes\n     \u00b2 Up to 100 global search restarts followed by 50 SLSQP optimizations\n     \u00b9 Track best independent run using the 100 best candidates\n   - Projection to feasible set through iterative averaging\n\n2. Representation and transforms:\n   - Default: real-valued array of length n\n   - Fourier basis: \n        H_hat = (1/n) * np.fourier(h, hanning window)\n   - Polynomial basis:\n        h_poly = np.polynomial.legendre.legfit(x, n_orders)\n\n3. Algorithm sequencing:\n   - Initialize population from multiple methods\n   - Apply local covariance correction: Whiteley projection\n   - Local descent: Exact L-BFGS-B with bound constraints\n   - Final repair: mosek_apnl solver for constrained optimization\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\nWe maintain:\n- Population size: 200 for each resolution\n- Mutation amplitude: 0.03 - 0.05\n- Crossover rate: 0.7\n- Inertia weight: 0.7 to 0.4 (cosine adjusted)\n\nNumerical robustness and memory use\n- Maximum n=3040 (approx 12MB storage)\n- Quadruple redundancy in evaluation (every candidate retested)\n- Gradient estimation via centered differences\n- Iterative repair with projection matrix for boundary handling\n\nRuntimesafeguardsandreturncontract\nImplementation will:\n* Verify h \u2208 [0,1]^n and sum=1 always\n* Compute objective via standard evaluator\n* Include automatic solution repair using:\n   - Projection algorithm\n   - Random resampling with annealing\n* Return best solution found and its objective\n\nPseudocode structure:\n1. Parameterize n_grid and restart schemes\n2. Define repair_helper(h) ensuring feasibility\n3. Initialize with multiple diverse methods\n4. For each generation:\n   a. Apply mutation/mixing\n   b. Evaluate using max_correlation dx factor\n   c. Update best\n   d. Elitism ensures solution continuity\n5. Local search via L-BFGS-B\n6. Return best solution and objective",
